km" [:br {}] "Intermediate 2: 15.0 km" [:br {}] "Intermediate 3: 20.5 km" [:br {}] "Maximum Climb: 51 m" [:br {}] "Total Climbing: 1,126 m"]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "This was the final women’s event of the 2002 cross-country program. The event had not been held at the 2001 World Championships, when it was cancelled because of weather conditions. The best female cross-country skier of the past five years had been Norwegian " [:a {:href "/athletes/81768"} "Bente Skari-Martinsen"] ", but she was better in the shorter distances. The early leader in this race was Russian " [:a {:href "/athletes/81634"} "Larisa Lazutina"] ", the 1999 World Champion and 2001 Holmenkollen champion in the event. And she was the late leader too, winning by almost two minutes over Italy’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/82035"} "Gabriella Paruzzi"] ". Lazutina led at every checkpoint and had the fastest intermediate split for each time check. But she would not win the gold medal."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In December 2003 it was revealed that Lazutina had tested positive for darpopoietin, an erythropoietin analogue, and was disqualified from all events in Salt Lake City. Her teammate, " [:a {:href "/athletes/80915"} "Olga Danilova"] ", who had originally finished eighth had been discovered two months earlier and was also disqualified. This brought Paruzzi up to a very surprising gold medal, as she had never won an individual medal before at three Winter Olympics or the World Championships. The silver medalist became Italy " [:a {:href "/athletes/80627"} "Stefania Belmondo"] " while Skari-Martinsen won the bronze. For Belmondo this was her 10th Olympic medal, equaling the Winter Olympic record for women held by Soviet/Unified Team skier " [:a {:href "/athletes/82399"} "Raisa Smetanina"] ", both trailing only Norway’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/85378"} "Bjørn Dæhlie"] " among men, who won 12."] "\n"] [:button {:id "button935647", :class "btn btn-default"} "Details"] [:script {} "$('#button935647').cl